# Upload filter for the Vercel build. Root Directory is the repo root (so that `vercel.json` beside
# this file is the one Vercel reads), which means the whole monorepo is in scope — and almost none of
# it builds the site.
#
# Not a security control: the funded keypairs in `.devnet/` are gitignored, so a git-based deploy
# never sees them at all. This is about not shipping a Rust workspace to a Node build box.

programs/

# engine/ is 85MB and none of it runs the site — EXCEPT engine/src, which the build genuinely needs.
# `er-demo/src/sim/mirrorParity.test.ts` imports `../../../engine/src/er-sim.ts` to assert the
# client's fight simulation still matches the engine's, and `tsc -b` type-checks it, so excluding the
# whole directory failed the build with "Cannot find module" and nothing else to go on.
#
# Keep the negation rather than dropping the test from the typecheck: that parity check is the thing
# standing between the browser's replay and the engine's, and it costs 668K to keep honest.
engine/*
!engine/src

web/
shared/
sandbox/
# ANCHORED, and the leading slash is load-bearing. These patterns are gitignore syntax, so a bare
# `scripts/` matches a directory of that name at ANY depth — including `er-demo/scripts/`, which the
# build now needs. Deleting the explicit `er-demo/scripts/` line below did nothing for exactly that
# reason: this line was still catching it, one directory deeper and silently.
/scripts/
target/
pitch/

# er-demo/scripts/ IS UPLOADED, and it is the one entry here that ships something the site does not
# run. 1.1 MB across 45 files, and the reason is divergence rather than size.
#
# `npm run build` is `tsc -b && vite build`, and `tsc -b` builds every project the root
# `tsconfig.json` references. When `tsconfig.scripts.json` was added to that list — closing a gap
# where the entire keeper had never been type-checked — the next deploy failed with
# `TS18003: No inputs were found in config file 'tsconfig.scripts.json'`, because this file was
# hiding the directory it points at. The build box and the developer's machine were type-checking
# different trees, which is precisely the class of bug that had just been fixed one layer up.
#
# The alternative was to narrow the build's project list so it checks only what deploys. That
# re-creates the divergence deliberately: a change that compiles on Vercel and not locally, or the
# reverse, and nothing to say which is authoritative. One tree, one answer, 1.1 MB.
er-demo/design/
er-demo/dist/

node_modules/
.DS_Store
